able regions.\n\nIn hg18, chr20 starts from a different position — hg18 chr20 is 62,435,964 bp. In hg38, chr20 is 64,444,167 bp and starts with a ~128kb gap? Actually, GRCh38 chr20: positions 1-128085? Hmm, no. Does GRCh38.p13 chr20 start with a 128 kb gap? I recall that GRCh38 chr20 positions 1-128085 were the p-arm heterochromatin... actually, in GRCh38 the p-arm of chr20 starts from a centromeric gap. Hmm.\n\nAnyway, the correct approach is: for each reference, load the chr20 sequence and check every VCF record's REF allele against the base at the declared 1-based coordinate. Count matches/mismatches per build. This is deterministic.\n\nThe FASTA file: gzipped, probably contains one sequence with a header line like \">chr20\" or \">20\". Let me check the header.\n\nAlso, I need to handle multi-line FASTA: either read the whole thing and strip newlines, or build an index. Files are ~18-19 MB compressed, ~62-64 MB uncompressed — loading into memory as a Python string is no problem (3 files = ~190 MB, no issue).\n\nAlso, consider that VCF records may contain indels: the REF of an indel should match the reference base at that position. For multiallelic sites, check the REF.\n\nAdditional evidence beyond REF matching: compare match rates across builds, and if there's a tie, do a liftover-style check. But since the references are provided, REF match rate is the definitive coordinate check.\n\nAlso, to distinguish from T2T: T2T (hs1) chr20 is 66,210,255 bp and includes the full p-arm and acrocentric sequences, so the coordinate system differs from hg38 by ~2 Mb. Without a T2T reference file we can't directly check, but the README says T2T is excluded as a candidate because there's no file, and the final call requires one of the provided references to overwhelmingly dominate. I can add supporting evidence: e.g., the max POS in the VCF vs. chromosome length; and if hg19 matches at ~100%, that settles it.\n\nPlan: write output/analysis.py that does the following:\n1. Parse